Files substantially derived from pigeons, in descending order of how much
upstream code remains:

    src/service/windows.rs            Windows service backend, near verbatim
    src/service/windows/firewall.rs   Windows firewall rules, near verbatim
    src/service/macos.rs              launchd backend, renamed and reworked
    src/service/linux.rs              systemd backend, plus a new user-unit path
    src/service.rs                    service dispatch, plus ticket publishing
    src/keys.rs                       z32 key load/store, from upstream ssh.rs
    src/tunnel.rs                     endpoint builder and TCP/QUIC bridging
    src/protocol.rs                   the flushing copy loop
    src/main.rs                       CLI skeleton and service subcommands
    .github/workflows/ci.yml          CI matrix
    Makefile.toml                     cargo-make tasks
    service/*.sh                      service install and uninstall scripts

Files written for remoteadb, with no upstream equivalent:

    src/adb.rs                        adb server supervision
    src/config.rs                     layered TOML config, allowlist, saved hosts
    src/host.rs                       host role wiring and allowlist reload
    deployment/*                      private iroh-relay configuration
    Makefile                          build conventions

Summary of changes made to the derived files:

  * The tunnel targets a local adb server rather than sshd, and the host
    supervises `adb nodaemon server` instead of merely probing for a daemon.
  * Connections are gated by an endpoint-ID allowlist, checked immediately
    after the QUIC handshake. Upstream needed no such check because sshd
    authenticates its own callers; adb has no authentication at all.
  * Endpoint discovery is disabled. Upstream builds endpoints with
    presets::N0, which publishes to and resolves through n0's DNS and pkarr
    servers even when custom relays are configured. remoteadb uses
    presets::Minimal and requires an explicit relay URL, so no endpoint
    information leaves the configured relays.
  * Peers are addressed by ticket (endpoint ID plus relay URL) rather than
    by bare endpoint ID, which discovery would otherwise have to resolve.
  * The client holds one QUIC connection per host and opens a stream per
    TCP connection, where upstream dialed a new connection each time.
  * SSH config file manipulation and the ProxyCommand stdio mode were
    removed, replaced by a named-host list and a local TCP listener.
  * iroh-services telemetry was removed entirely.
  * The Linux service backend gained a per-user systemd unit.
  * The iroh dependency was updated from 1.0 to 1.3.

This product depends on iroh, also by N0, INC., obtained from crates.io under
MIT OR Apache-2.0. See Cargo.toml and Cargo.lock for the full dependency set
and its versions.
